    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d519">

                <pb facs="1401" n="1395" sameAs="frus:frus1894app2/pg1395"/>

                <head>No. 5.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Gresham to Mr. Thurston.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1895-01-20"><hi rendition="#italic">January 20,
                        1895</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note of this date, in which, referring to the uprising in Hawaii, you
                    say that although your Government is and will continue in the future, as it has
                    in the past, fully able to maintain itself against the attacks of all domestic
                    enemies, in view of the serious nature of the charges now pending against a
                    large number of both foreigners and natives, and of other complicating
                    conditions, the state of affairs at the islands is critical, and that, owing to
                    its limited police and military force, your Government may not be able, in
                    sudden contingencies, to afford protection to foreign citizens and their
                    property for the time being.</p>
                <p>On receipt of a dispatch from Mr. Willis yesterday morning on the subject of the
                    uprising, which was immediately given to the press, a war ship was at once
                    ordered to proceed from San Francisco to Honolulu to afford needed protection to
                    any Americans entitled to it. That fact was also announced by the press Saturday
                    afternoon and this morning, and I naturally supposed that the announcement had
                    attracted your notice.</p>
                <p>Accept, sir, the renewed assurances of my highest consideration.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. Q. Gresham.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
